WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Hi, there's Steve Kaufmann here,
and today I wanna talk again about

00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:09.480
basics and explain why we didn't
bother trying to master the basics

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.010
of the language we're learning.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:15.660
I talked a bit about the basics
last time, or in a previous video.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:20.490
I explained that there is sort of
a, a theory which I, uh, basically,

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:24.960
uh, subscribe to, and that is that
different aspects of the language,

00:00:24.965 --> 00:00:26.280
we're gonna learn them in a certain.

00:00:27.435 --> 00:00:30.195
to some extent it depends on
what our native language is.

00:00:30.195 --> 00:00:32.265
It depends on the language we're learning.

00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:36.045
It depends on what kind of
content we're exposing ourselves

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:37.905
to, but we can't force it.

00:00:38.055 --> 00:00:40.785
Things will fall into place
when they fall into place.

00:00:40.785 --> 00:00:46.455
So don't worry about the basics, don't
worry about, you know, when you're

00:00:46.455 --> 00:00:50.595
quite well along in the language and
there's still certain basic terms or

00:00:50.595 --> 00:00:52.754
basic structures that you get wrong.

00:00:53.324 --> 00:00:58.305
Uh, don't try to, you know, force the
pace by learning the basics up front.

00:00:58.305 --> 00:01:00.295
I, I think it's a futile exercise.

00:01:00.644 --> 00:01:02.805
And so I want to get
into more detail on this.

00:01:02.805 --> 00:01:04.485
First of all, what are the basics?

00:01:04.935 --> 00:01:08.835
If I Google, uh, you know, the basics.

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:11.535
The basics of Spanish, the
basics of French, the basics of

00:01:11.535 --> 00:01:13.425
Russian, of Chinese, of Japanese.

00:01:13.725 --> 00:01:18.164
There's all kinds of pages on the
internet, which will give you various

00:01:18.195 --> 00:01:20.340
versions of what the basics are.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:25.290
They could be basic vocabulary and some
will say, you know, you gotta learn

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:29.370
the numbers and you gotta learn the
colors and the names of the months.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.960
And all of these things are very, or names
of different, you know, food products.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.200
All of these things are
very difficult to remember.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:42.450
I find that numbers and colors take
a long time before they sink in and,

00:01:42.509 --> 00:01:48.149
and studying a list of numbers or
days of the month, uh, or excuse me,

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:51.420
of months, et cetera, names of the
months is, is not gonna help you.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.289
You have to kind of learn them
gradually as you're exposing

00:01:55.295 --> 00:01:57.060
yourself to more and more input.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.940
Some people say the basics are,
you know, hello, how are you?

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:01.920
My name is...

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.985
Those kinds of phrases,
greetings and so forth.

00:02:06.285 --> 00:02:11.445
But a lot of these things you're gonna
learn fairly soon in the process anyway.

00:02:11.535 --> 00:02:15.555
One of the reasons why we designed
our mini stories at LingQ with

00:02:15.555 --> 00:02:22.395
high frequency verbs is because we
need verbs and high frequency verbs

00:02:22.455 --> 00:02:24.075
are quite important for speaking.

00:02:24.075 --> 00:02:27.075
Those are the ones we're going
to use when we start to speak.

00:02:27.345 --> 00:02:31.590
And in the mini stories, there's
a lot of repetition uh, in each

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:37.170
story of high frequency verbs and a
across the story, there's a lot of

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.210
repetition of high frequency verbs.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:44.550
I, I even went to a ChatGPT I asked,
what are the basics in Spanish?

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:51.000
They gave me a list of 10 things,
uh, one of which was wrong.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:56.440
You know, it says, okay, word order
in Spanish is subject-verb-object.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.700
Again, we're gonna very quickly
discover the verb object.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:03.990
We can look at it ahead of time as part
of an overview of the language, but

00:03:03.995 --> 00:03:08.850
very soon, if we're exposing ourselves
to content, and I, as you know, I

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:10.490
recommend our mini stories at LingQ.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:14.250
You're gonna come across that it's
gonna seem natural to you that that's

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.010
how they do things in that language.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.900
Over time, it'll become
the dominant pattern.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.620
Gender, Spanish nouns have a
gender, masculine or feminine.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.480
Yeah, you learn that pretty quickly.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.590
That doesn't mean you know
which nouns are which gender.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:34.920
Uh, five, of course, Ser and the estar,
uh, is, is this aspect, basic, call

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.435
it basic in Spanish, that the verb
to be can either temporary status

00:03:39.435 --> 00:03:43.425
or a permanent status, but there's
a fair amount of subjectivity there.

00:03:44.625 --> 00:03:47.955
And the interesting thing about Ser
and estar is you can get it wrong

00:03:47.955 --> 00:03:49.605
and it doesn't hinder communication.

00:03:49.605 --> 00:03:54.705
So to what extent is that basic
to comprehension or communication?

00:03:54.705 --> 00:03:56.565
I don't think it's that important.

00:03:56.565 --> 00:03:58.995
I think we gradually
get more and more used.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:05.390
Uh, ChatGPT also told me that personal
subject pronouns, Spanish has personal

00:04:05.395 --> 00:04:11.220
subject pronouns such as ...which must
be used in conjunction with verbs.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:16.440
That, of course, is not true because in
Spain, in Spanish, the verbs conjugate.

00:04:16.445 --> 00:04:17.490
The endings change.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.940
So very often you can tell which
person that is without having

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.370
to use the personal pronoun.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.870
But that's ChatGPT.

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.650
That's not necessarily
an authoritative source.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:34.905
Um, but then you can go to, I went to
another source and here are the basic,

00:04:35.025 --> 00:04:37.815
you know, topics of grammar for French.

00:04:38.505 --> 00:04:41.865
And it has terms in here, like
aspects, habitual perfective,

00:04:41.865 --> 00:04:46.245
imperfection, mood indicative,
imperative, conditional subjunctive.

00:04:46.905 --> 00:04:53.395
Uh, you know, if you try to learn that
upfront, uh, you know, conjunctions,

00:04:53.635 --> 00:04:55.985
coordinating conjunctions,
subordinating conjunctions.

00:04:57.075 --> 00:04:59.294
I just think it would be very
difficult to learn that upfront.

00:04:59.864 --> 00:05:03.465
So I don't think you can learn
basic vocabulary upfront.

00:05:03.734 --> 00:05:06.434
I don't think you can learn
the basic structures upfront.

00:05:06.945 --> 00:05:10.695
I think, as I've said many times,
language learning comes down to the

00:05:10.695 --> 00:05:14.804
three keys that, again, I can't remember
her name, but the lady who ran the,

00:05:15.284 --> 00:05:19.484
uh, San Diego State, uh, Language
Department said it's all about your

00:05:19.489 --> 00:05:23.629
attitude, the time you spend with the
language and your ability to notice.

00:05:24.729 --> 00:05:26.115
And so attitude is very important.

00:05:26.925 --> 00:05:28.515
Of course, we want to improve.

00:05:29.145 --> 00:05:34.815
Uh, I'm not suggesting we should
ignore, uh, correct, correct usage.

00:05:34.815 --> 00:05:37.815
I mean, this depends as well,
but obviously we have a model of

00:05:37.815 --> 00:05:41.685
the language that we would like
to get as close as possible to.

00:05:41.985 --> 00:05:46.365
So we want to be constantly
improving, constantly getting close

00:05:46.365 --> 00:05:48.195
to this model of the language.

00:05:48.435 --> 00:05:53.295
Now it could be Brazilian usage in
Portuguese or usage in Portugal.

00:05:53.295 --> 00:05:57.315
It could be usage in Quebec
for French or usage in France.

00:05:57.315 --> 00:06:02.055
It could be, um, usage in Taiwan
for Chinese or usage in Beijing.

00:06:02.715 --> 00:06:07.335
We have a model of the language which
we like, and, and the more we like it,

00:06:07.685 --> 00:06:10.455
the more we're likely to imitate it.

00:06:11.055 --> 00:06:13.275
Imitation is the highest form of flattery.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:17.235
We want to get closer to that usage,
and so we start to notice more things.

00:06:17.414 --> 00:06:19.965
The more we listen, the
more things we notice.

00:06:20.085 --> 00:06:23.325
Things that we don't notice at
first, we will notice them later on.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:29.969
The idea and, and so I think there was a
sort of pushback from some people saying,

00:06:29.969 --> 00:06:32.099
you know, how can you ignore the basics?

00:06:32.099 --> 00:06:35.969
Uh uh, we have to have the basics,
or we can't speak correctly.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:36.900
That's not true.

00:06:37.169 --> 00:06:41.760
I have tried mastering tables, you
know, declension tables in German

00:06:41.765 --> 00:06:43.109
and so forth, and it didn't work.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:47.460
It was only when I got into more and
more listening and reading, exposed my,

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:52.320
exposing myself to the language that
some of these things started to slot in.

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:55.219
So we have to have an attitude
of wanting to improve.

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:58.380
If we don't want to
improve, we won't notice.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.000
So even in those three keys, obviously
attitude affects the other two.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:06.240
If you like the language, you're
gonna spend time with the language.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:09.840
If you like the language and you want
to notice things in the language,

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:13.770
you'll notice more things in the
language, whether that be pronunciation

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:16.980
or usage or whatever it may be.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:23.390
Something like the subjunctive, I don't
think we notice it at first, but in

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:28.080
time, if we're motivated, if we like
the language, if we're hearing someone

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.250
speak the language in a way that we like
and we want to imitate, we're going to

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:40.020
imitate the subjunctive, but not using the
subjunctive doesn't hinder communication.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:43.860
So it's not a basic, in the sense
that it prevents communication.

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:48.330
It is a basic in the sense that we
would like to get as close as possible

00:07:48.335 --> 00:07:50.970
to this model of usage that we have.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:56.050
Uh, so I, I just wanted to sort
of follow up on my previous video.

00:07:56.190 --> 00:08:00.090
I didn't wanna suggest that we don't
want to speak, you know, correctly,

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:06.000
or we don't want to, you know, imitate
certain standard patterns of usage.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:11.580
I think every serious language learner
wants to do that, but achieving that

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:16.979
will depend on having an attitude
that I like the language, I enjoy

00:08:16.979 --> 00:08:18.690
the language, I enjoy learning.

00:08:18.695 --> 00:08:19.680
I want to get better.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:22.860
I want to get closer to
that standard of usage.

00:08:22.979 --> 00:08:24.419
I'm willing to put in the time.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:26.140
There are no shortcuts.

00:08:26.145 --> 00:08:28.500
It's not as if we can learn a formula.

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:31.605
This is how the language works,
now we're going to be able to

00:08:31.605 --> 00:08:32.925
learn the language more quickly.

00:08:33.135 --> 00:08:37.095
I don't believe that we can have an
overview if we want, where it tells us

00:08:37.095 --> 00:08:42.945
that this language is subject-verb-object
or subjec-object-verb, sort of

00:08:42.945 --> 00:08:44.925
an abstract of the language.

00:08:44.985 --> 00:08:46.725
I'm not sure that helps very much.

00:08:46.755 --> 00:08:52.830
Either we don't understand what that
overview actually is saying or we

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:56.700
will in any case, as with so many
of these sort of basic vocabulary

00:08:56.730 --> 00:09:02.130
items, basic structures, we will
eventually get those through a program

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:04.080
of sustained listening and reading.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:08.310
So, uh, because there was a
certain amount of pushback on this

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:09.900
idea of, of avoiding the basics

00:09:10.725 --> 00:09:14.415
uh, I thought I would follow up
with another video on the subject.

00:09:14.445 --> 00:09:18.255
By the way, with reference to
ChatGPT I am looking at it.

00:09:18.555 --> 00:09:24.105
I don't know yet where that fits into
my language learning routine or possibly

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:30.695
with, with LingQ, I'm going to be doing
a video, uh, uh, interview with Luca

00:09:30.695 --> 00:09:35.025
Lampariello, and I think we may even
discuss that subject a little bit.

00:09:35.145 --> 00:09:36.925
So thank you for listening.

00:09:37.195 --> 00:09:37.755
Bye for now.

